  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two related photocatalytic or semiconductor reaction schemes inside spherical particles, emphasizing how heat and gas atmosphere change defect chemistry and charge-carrier behavior. In both spheres, heating is shown from the outside, while electrons and holes move in opposite directions through the particle. White dashed circles appear to represent vacancies or defect sites, and blue circles indicate atoms or ions occupying those sites.\n\nA three-step sequence is marked in each particle. First, heat seems to drive migration or formation of internal defects and mobile species. Second, these defects move toward the surface and interact with charge carriers. Third, the surrounding gas reversibly exchanges with the solid: on the left, hydrogen is converted to water at the surface, while on the right, oxygen participates in a corresponding surface exchange.\n\nThe main comparison is between a reducing H2/H2O environment and an oxidizing O2 environment. Under hydrogen, the diagram suggests reduction and oxygen removal, producing more vacancy-like sites. Under oxygen, the opposite process appears to refill vacancies and reoxidize the material. Overall, the image conveys a reversible, heat-assisted redox cycle linking bulk defect migration, surface reactions, and electron–hole transport.",
